<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext ws:// and http:// to the house.

  Valar terminates no TLS: it is a LAN and tailnet appliance, reached by IP or
  by a tailnet name, and there is no certificate to present. Android blocks
  cleartext by default since API 28, which is what the Echo Show client hit as
  "CLEARTEXT communication to 10.1.95.5 not permitted".

  This permits cleartext GLOBALLY rather than per domain, and that is
  deliberate after getting it wrong once: a domain entry matches a DNS name,
  never a CIDR range, so listing "10.0.0.0" with includeSubdomains does NOT
  cover 10.1.95.5. It covers hostnames ending in ".10.0.0.0", which is
  nothing. The first cut of this file did exactly that, and the client refused
  its own house while every other device on the network reached it fine.

  A per-domain list cannot express "whatever address this operator's house is
  at", which is the actual requirement: the address is typed in at first run
  and can be any private IP or any tailnet name. When Valar grows TLS this
  becomes a base-config of false with the house's name as the exception.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
